---
import DeepNav from '../../../components/DeepNav.astro';
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import MainFindings from '../../../components/MainFindings.astro';
import Chapter3Intro from '../../../components/Chapter3Intro.astro';
import full from '../../../data/trust-in-medical-ai/study4-full.json';
import bayes from '../../../data/trust-in-medical-ai/bayes.json';
import { times } from '../../../utils/bayes';
import { href } from '../../../utils/url';
import { f1, f2, noZero, pEq, REPO } from '../../../utils/fmt';

const bt = (m: string, id: string) => (full.betweenTests as any)[m].find((t: any) => t.id === id);
const wt = (m: string, id: string) => (full.withinTests as any)[m].find((t: any) => t.id === id);
const bf = (id: string) => bayes.rows.find((r) => r.id === id)!;
const eff = (m: string) => full.effectCompare.find((e: any) => e.measure === m)!;
const n = full.sample.n;
const bHS = bt('trust', 'hum-std'), bAD = bt('trust', 'ai-doc');
const wHS = wt('trust', 'hum-std'), wDS = wt('trust', 'doc-std'), wDH = wt('trust', 'doc-hum');
const wWarm = wt('warmth', 'hum-std'), wComp = wt('competence', 'hum-std');
const check = full.manipulationCheck;
const models = (full.mixedModels as any).trust;
const wa = (full.withinAnova as any).trust;

const findings = [
  {
    head: 'Seen alone, the voiced humanlike AI still did not win more trust',
    body: [
      `${n} adults first read and rated one recommender: a doctor, a standard AI or a humanlike AI, each with a voice reading the advice. The humanlike AI scored ${f2(bt('trust', 'hum-std').meanA)} on trust and the standard AI ${f2(bt('trust', 'hum-std').meanB)}.`,
      `The difference is small (d = ${f2(bHS.d)}, ${pEq(bHS.p)}). The data are ${times(bf('S4-trust-hum-std').bf01)} under no difference, which is anecdotal, not firm evidence.`,
    ],
    visual: { type: 'stat', value: `d = ${f2(bHS.d)}`, label: 'humanlike AI against standard AI, each rated alone', caption: 'Study 4, Part 1.' },
  },
  {
    head: 'Side by side, the humanlike AI pulled clearly ahead of the standard AI',
    body: [
      `Everyone then rated all three recommenders. In this comparison the humanlike AI was trusted ${f1(wHS.diff)} points more than the standard AI, ${f2(wHS.meanA)} against ${f2(wHS.meanB)} (d = ${f2(wHS.d)}, p < .001).`,
      `${wHS.higher} of ${wHS.n} people trusted the humanlike AI more, ${wHS.same} trusted both the same, and ${wHS.lower} trusted it less.`,
    ],
    visual: { type: 'stat', value: `+${f1(wHS.diff)} points`, label: 'more trust in the humanlike AI than in the standard AI, among the same people', caption: `Trust runs from −3 to 3. ${wHS.n} people in Part 2.` },
  },
  {
    head: 'It nearly closed the gap to the doctor',
    body: [
      `The doctor was trusted ${f1(wDS.diff)} points more than the standard AI, but only ${f2(wDH.diff)} more than the humanlike AI (${f2(wDH.meanA)} against ${f2(wDH.meanB)}). The gap in effect size shrank from d = ${f2(wDS.d)} to ${f2(wDH.d)}.`,
      `The data are only ${times(bf('S4w-trust-hum-doc').bf01)} under no difference between the doctor and the humanlike AI, so the gap is small, not proven absent.`,
    ],
    visual: { type: 'pairs', aName: 'Compared with the standard AI', bName: 'Compared with the humanlike AI', rows: [{ label: 'Doctor advantage in trust (d)', a: wDS.d, b: wDH.d, max: 0.5 }], caption: 'Doctor minus AI, side by side. Study 4, Part 2.' },
  },
  {
    head: 'Comparing changed how people judged the AI',
    body: [
      `The same humanlike-versus-standard contrast was d = ${f2(eff('trust').between['hum-std'].d)} when each person saw one, and d = ${f2(eff('trust').within['hum-std'].d)} when they saw all three. For belief it went from ${f2(eff('belief').between['hum-std'].d)} to ${f2(eff('belief').within['hum-std'].d)}.`,
      'Part 1 and Part 2 use different formulas for d, so I read the pattern, not the exact ratio. The shift is large enough to matter either way.',
    ],
    visual: { type: 'pairs', aName: 'Seen alone', bName: 'Side by side', rows: [
      { label: 'Trust', a: eff('trust').between['hum-std'].d, b: eff('trust').within['hum-std'].d, max: 0.5 },
      { label: 'Belief', a: eff('belief').between['hum-std'].d, b: eff('belief').within['hum-std'].d, max: 0.5 },
    ], caption: 'Humanlike AI against standard AI, effect size (d).' },
  },
  {
    head: 'Warmth and competence carried the effect',
    body: [
      `Side by side, the humanlike AI was rated warmer (d = ${f2(wWarm.d)}) and more competent (d = ${f2(wComp.d)}) than the standard AI. When I put warmth and competence into the model, the humanlike advantage over the standard AI went from ${f2(-models[0].terms[1].b)} to ${f2(-models[2].terms[1].b)} points.`,
      'That pattern fits the idea that people trusted the humanlike AI more because it seemed warmer and more capable.',
    ],
    visual: { type: 'stat', value: `${f2(-models[0].terms[1].b)} to ${f2(-models[2].terms[1].b)}`, label: 'humanlike advantage over the standard AI in trust points, before and after accounting for warmth and competence', caption: 'Study 4, Part 2 mixed model.' },
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Trust in medical AI: Study 4"
  description="Study 4 of my dissertation: does a humanlike AI with a voice win more trust, alone and when compared with a doctor and a standard AI?"
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="page-head">
      <p class="back small"><a href={href('/projects/trust-in-medical-ai/')}>Trust in medical AI</a></p>
      <h1>Study 4</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        {n} adults read a health recommendation, with a voice reading it aloud, from a doctor, a standard AI or a humanlike AI. Then everyone rated all three recommenders in a random order.
        That gives two tests of the same idea: one when people judged a single recommender, and one when they compared. This page gives the main findings and what they mean.
        Every table and figure is on the next page, rebuilt from the released data.
      </p>
    </header>
    <Chapter3Intro current="4" />
    <DeepNav study="4" current="overview" />

    <section class="section" id="main-findings" aria-labelledby="mf-title">
      <h2 id="mf-title">Main findings</h2>
      <MainFindings findings={findings} />
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="implications" aria-labelledby="implications-title">
      <h2 id="implications-title">What this means</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          <strong>Humanlike framing helps through comparison.</strong> With one recommender at a time, the effect was small and uncertain, as in Study 3. When people could weigh the three against each other,
          the humanlike AI came out clearly ahead of the standard one. A person judging an AI on its own has little to anchor on. Next to alternatives, the cues in the humanlike version become visible.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The effect is about impressions of warmth and competence.</strong> The humanlike AI looked warmer and more competent, and those ratings accounted for the whole advantage in the model. This is a statistical
          account, not proof of mechanism, but it points to what to measure in a real product: how capable and warm an AI seems to its users.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The check worked this time.</strong> People rated the humanlike AI as more humanlike than the standard one (d = {f2(check.d)}, {pEq(check.p)}), which Study 3 had not shown. That supports reading the Part 1
          result as a weak effect, not a failed manipulation.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>A correction to the dissertation.</strong> The dissertation reports the omnibus repeated-measures test of trust as F(2, 1573) = 1.65, p = .192. I could not reproduce that. A repeated-measures ANOVA on the {wa.n} people who rated all three gives
          F({wa.df1}, {wa.df2}) = {f2(wa.F)}, p &lt; .001, which agrees with the pairwise tests. The pairwise results and every effect size elsewhere are unaffected.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Cautions.</strong> Part 2 reuses the people from Part 1, and everyone who rated all three had already formed a first impression. The order was randomized, but I did not test order effects here.
          The advice was a single scenario, and trust is stated trust, not behavior.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="files" aria-labelledby="files-title">
      <h2 id="files-title">Files and methods</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <ul class="filelist">
          <li><a href={href('/data/study4-ratings.csv')}>study4-ratings.csv</a>, the de-identified ratings, one row per person</li>
          <li><a href={href('/data/study4-codebook.csv')}>study4-codebook.csv</a>, what every column means</li>
          <li><a href={href('/code/study4-analysis.R')}>study4-analysis.R</a>, the main tests in R, from the public file</li>
          <li><a href={href('/code/study4-original-analysis.Rmd')}>study4-original-analysis.Rmd</a>, my original notebook for this study, with local file paths removed</li>
          <li><a href="https://doi.org/10.17605/OSF.IO/C6RMT">Preregistration and materials on OSF</a>, written before data collection</li>
          <li><a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/analysis/study4-full-results.py`}>study4-full-results.py</a>, the code behind every table and figure</li>
          <li><a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/analysis/study4-deidentify.py`}>study4-deidentify.py</a>, how the raw export became the public file</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Everything on these pages was computed in Python from the released file. Part 1 uses Welch t tests and one-way ANOVAs with Tukey adjustment, with d from the pooled standard deviation. Part 2 uses
          paired t tests with a Bonferroni correction, dz as the effect size, a repeated-measures ANOVA with the Greenhouse-Geisser correction, and mixed models with a random intercept for each person.
          The released file has {n} people and shuffled row order, with no IDs, locations, timestamps or free text, and age in four bands.
        </p>
        <p>
          The dissertation reports Cohen's d as 2t divided by the square root of the degrees of freedom. These pages use the pooled standard deviation for Part 1, which gives somewhat different values from the dissertation's
          for some tests. The dissertation also lists a sample of 556 people where the released file has {n}; I use the file.
        </p>
        <p class="small muted">
          The data, codebook and code are shared under <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">CC BY 4.0</a> (data) and the MIT license (code). Please do not use the file to try to identify anyone.
          Please cite the dissertation: Leitao, M. R. (2024). <em>Artificial Intelligence, Humanization, and Health Beliefs</em>. Georgetown University.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .filelist { padding-left: 1.2rem; font-family: var(--display); }
  .filelist li { margin-bottom: 0.3rem; }
  section :global(h2) { margin-bottom: 0.75rem; }
</style>
